<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hildburghausen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hildburghausen is a small town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, near the Bavarian border. From 1680 to 1826 it was the residence of the dukes of Saxe-Hildburghausen, whose daughter Therese married the future King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Ludwig I of Bavaria</a> in 1810. After the duchy was merged into Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, the publisher Joseph Meyer ran his Bibliographisches Institut there from 1828 to 1874.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hildburghausen.jpg" alt="Hildburghausen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernd Hutschenreuther · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hildburghausen_Historisches_Rathaus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
